  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nCurrently, an outflow boundary is moving south across the southern \nhalf of the Tennessee valley and southern Plateau. Scattered storms \ncontinue to develop along this boundary. MLCAPES of 2500 and DCAPES \nof 900-1100. Microbursts parameter of 5-7 so isolated damaging winds \nfrom these storms are possible.\n\nSatellite and SPC meso-analysis shows airmass has stabilized over \neast Kentucky, northern half of Tennessee, southwest Virginia so \nexpect these areas to remain dry the rest of today.\n\nAreas along and south of the outflow boundary remain very hot with\nheat indices over 100 degrees. North of the boundary conditions\nare much better. However, given the outside events we will\ncontinue through expiration. \n\nMost clear sky, calm wind, and recent rains will allow for areas of \nfog development tonight some dense, especially near area waterways.\n\nFor Sunday, another MCV or short-wave will move southeast across the \narea. Another round of scattered showers and thunderstorms \nanticipated mainly along and east of interstate 75. Airmass will be \nfavorable for localized damaging winds, frequent lightning and \nlocally heavy rains.\n\nFor Monday, an inverted surface trough across the Plateau and \nwestern Tennessee valley will support isolated convection mainly \nalong and west of interstate 40. Again, strong/gusty winds and \nfrequent lightning will be the main concern.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, upper ridging builds back over the \nTennessee valley and southern Appalachians producing hot and dry \nconditions. Dewpoints will be in the middle and upper 60s which will \nlimit the heat indices below heat advisory criteria.\n\nThe upper ridge flattens for Thursday as a short-waves and frontal \nboundary approaches the area. Scattered showers and storms are \nexpected. The boundary moves south of the area for Friday.\n\nFor Saturday, return southerly flow around surface ridge east of the \nAppalachians will bring moisture and instability back into the area.",
